Currently `as_tibble.data.frame()` treats all extended data frames (class = <mydfclass, data.frame>) as if they were normal data frames (class = <data.frame>) and in turn ignores any `as.data.frame()` method that the object has available. A consequence of this manifested in  https://github.com/tidyverse/tibble/issues/1555, and the follow up report in https://github.com/Rdatatable/data.table/issues/5698, where additional attributes (only of use to {data.table}) were not removed on coercion.

In https://github.com/Rdatatable/data.table/issues/5698, Jan proposed the following small addition as a possible solution:

```R
as_tibble.data.frame = function(x, ...) {
  if (!identical(class(x), "data.frame")) return(as_tibble(as.data.frame(x)))
  ... # continue old as_tibble.data.frame body
}
```
This benefits not just {data.table} but any package that provides an extended object class. The onus is still on those packages to provide an `as.data.frame()` method but hopefully this is something they are already doing.

Happy to send a PR if receptive?

By following the principles defined at http://adv-r.hadley.nz/s3.html#inheritance

* Provide a constructor that can also be used to create subclassed objects

    ```R
    new_tibble <- function(data, ..., subclass = NULL) {
      stopifnot(is.data.frame(data))
      
      structure(
        data,
        ...,
        class = c(subclass, "tibble", "tbl_df", "data.frame")
      )
    }
   ```

* Provide a reconstruct method

   ```R
    reconstruct.tibble <- function(new, old) {
      new_tibble(new)
    }
    ```

* Call `S3::reconstruct()` in all methods that return a tibble

This supersedes #155, #211, #218.

lst used inside "if else" does not behave the same way.

AddModel<-function(Models,New)
{
    ModelList=lst({{New}})
  tibble(ModelList)%>%mutate(Name=names(ModelList))->AddedModels
}
Works as expected.

However, when "lst" is in the context of "if else", names are changed

AddModels<-function(Models,New)
{
    if(class(New)[1]!="list") 
      {ModelList=lst({{New}})}else{ModelList=New}
  tibble(ModelList)%>%mutate(Name=names(ModelList))->AddedModels 
}
Does not work.

If we "cheat" and move lst, then all is good again =

AddModels<-function(Models,New)# 
{
  ModelList=lst({{New}})
    if(class(New)[1]!="list") 
    {
      tibble(ModelList)%>%mutate(Name=names(ModelList))->AddedModels    
          }else{
            ModelList=New
      tibble(ModelList)%>%mutate(Name=names(ModelList))->AddedModels    
      }
}
Works

As long as "lst" is outside the if else, all is good.

